    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. feels like there's a lot going on down there that we just can't see. how do i actually figure out if it's microbial immobilization or if i just need more raw nutrients? is there a specific test i should do to see what's happening with the microbes specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A specific microbial test is usually quite advanced, but you can get a good idea by looking at the **C:N ratio** of your compost and soil. If your compost has a very high Carbon-to-Nitrogen ratio (lots of woody material, for example), the microbes will aggressively pull nitrogen from the soil to break down that carbon. If the ratio is high, you need to add a quick-release nitrogen source like blood meal to \"feed\" the microbes while also feeding your kale. Otherwise, if the C:N is balanced, you might just need more readily available nutrients.",
      "source": "target",
